sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch10-04> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "A contractile fibre fixed at both ends on a curved surface first pulls itself into the shortest path on that surface before it can constrict the organ, so the muscle fibres of hollow organs such as the heart, stomach, arteries and intestine automatically lie along geodesics (annular and longitudinal in a simple tube).";
    dc:type "mathematical-law";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Muscle fibres on hollow organs arrange themselves along geodesic curves";
    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation> "fibre fixed at both ends on a curved surface takes the shortest path: \\kappa_g = 0";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "D’Arcy Wentworth Thompson, On Growth and Form (1st ed., Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1917), Chapter X, “A Parenthetic Note on Geodetics”; topic “Muscle fibres of hollow organs”. Scope: muscular walls of hollow and tubular organs (heart, stomach, blood vessels, gut, worm body wall)"
}
